1 OCCU Sheriff (Lensmann og bonde)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Etne-soga by Ståle Dyrvik, vol V "Folket. Grindheim sokn." p 156 (27)
2 CONT
2 CONT and from Etne-soga by Ståle Dyrvik, vol II Bygde- og gardssoge 1660-1800 ) page 431.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sheriff Christen Hansen Frette, born around 1720, married to a girl named Anna. Christen H. Frette descended from an old lineage of Sheriffs - most likely sheriffs and "sysselmenn" (whose job was to collect taxes for the king, an
2 CONC d had important roles in the legal system). Christen H. Frette was sheriff in Etne, and had a farm at Frette. He was a decent sheriff and had high interests in road building in Etne. The roads he built for the authorities are stil
2 CONC l present."
2 CONT
2 CONT "It can be noted that sheriff Christen H. Frette rode with horse from Frette to Åkra church once - most likely on a preaching sunday in Åkra. It was so cold that winter that the ice over the fjord Kyrping in Åkra could be walked o
2 CONC n."
2 CONT
2 CONT Christen had quite a few quarrels with his step-father, Nils, and they both ended up in court more than once in fights about land. Christen was said to have spoken freely on witchcraft and to have cursed accoring to Nils.  Source:
2 CONC  Etnesoga II.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Christen is said to have been a good sheriff. Although he was accused of being too friendly with the bottle while visiting different farms while on the office's missions. But this was just rumors. More importantly he was a stand 
2 CONC up guy who was liked by most people and he didn't abuse his position."
2 CONT
2 CONT He got good pay and lived at a nice farm, and he is mentioned to have 1117 rdlr in 06/13/1788. He even got payed full pension after he left his job due to old age. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Etnesoga II ( Etne-soga by Ståle Dyrvik, vol II Bygde- og gardssoge 1660-1800 ) page 431.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had five daughters named Siri, at least two died in a very young age while two others grew up. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Etnesoga II page 432-3.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gotskalk Halvardsson, b. 1735 at Tveito, d. 1802 at Skjold. He received the deed to the farm for 32 rdlr., in 1752. In 1756 he got married and took over from Tor Frette. He was a very good bear hunter. At the council in l763 he c
2 CONC ollected a bounty of 2 rdlr., for a large bear he had killed on the Oyfjell (the Oy mountain). In 1766, he came back with two thick skins. In 1775, he received another 4 rdlr., for two bears he had killed. Together with bailiff ''
2 CONC 'Kristen Frette''''''' he engaged in horsetrading on the Roldal market, but got into a dispute with his partner in 1779"
2 CONT
2 CONT On the origin of the name Frette: "Frétt is a word in norse religious practise that was used as a kind of prayer (to ask the gods for knowledge, to ask about the future or about the gods will) - (NG XI s.76). Olsen thinks the name
2 CONC  Frette is connected to this word, and states that both Hellaugsvatnet and Frette have stories of norse rituals." 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Lived at Frette Etnesoga V.  Frette bruk 6-7 p156 (27)
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Christen Hansen who moved to Frette in a young age, around year 1725, and the farm - farm number 63 brn.nr.7 - has been in the family since." - Tørres Frette, Sunnhordland Avis
2 CONT
2 CONT translated from Etnesoga II p 427 Frette
2 CONT
2 CONT http://kittymunson.com/index.php?page=frette-farm-etnesoga-ii
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the 1700s floods and avalanches damaged Frette several times. In 1743 the rivers dug out much of the farms best field. The river from Hellaug took out several bridges and destroyed the road to the summer farm. At the same time
2 CONC , two avalanches had come down by the so called Kleive-barn and destroyed both pasture and good hayfield. Both Klokkarteigen and Volme had  damages from flooding, and several smaller avalanches had destroyed most of these hayfield
2 CONC s. In 1747 this damage was so serious that the farms tax (the deserted farms included) was reduced from 6 to about 3 ½ laupar. The biggest reduction was on now part no 1-5. After the next accident in 1770 it was only Kristen Hanss
2 CONC on on the other part who applied for reduction. The water had rissen so high that his fields and meadows along the waterfront were spoiled. An avalanche from Fretshovda had ended up in his home field. He now got a reduction so tha
2 CONC t the two parts of Frette were again equal. The total tax was now on the small side of 3 laupar. It was only the half of the tax the farm had gotten in 1667.  The land rent was reduced accordingly."
